AIM: Visit product listing pages, extract product details and links, follow pagination, save to CSV.

CODE EXPLAINED
1. Imports: csv, requests, BeautifulSoup, urljoin
   requests downloads the page, BeautifulSoup parses the HTML, urljoin builds full URLs, csv saves output.

2. BASE = "http://books.toscrape.com/"; MAX_PAGES = 3
   Practice e-commerce site made for scraping. We crawl only 3 listing pages.

3. products = []; url = BASE
   products stores results. url is the current page to fetch (starts at home page).

4. for page in range(MAX_PAGES):
   Loop once per listing page.

5. resp = requests.get(url, timeout=10); resp.encoding = "utf-8"
   Downloads the HTML. timeout stops it hanging. Setting utf-8 avoids garbled characters (e.g. the pound sign).

6. soup = BeautifulSoup(resp.text, "html.parser")
   Parses the HTML text into a searchable tree.

7. for item in soup.select("article.product_pod"):
   CSS selector: every <article class="product_pod"> is one product card on the page.

8. Extraction inside the loop
   title  = item.h3.a["title"]            -> full book name (stored in the title attribute)
   link   = urljoin(url, item.h3.a["href"]) -> relative link made into a full URL
   price  = item.select_one("p.price_color").text.strip()
   stock  = item.select_one("p.availability").text.strip()
   rating = item.select_one("p.star-rating")["class"][1] -> the class list is ["star-rating", "Three"], so index 1 gives "Three".
   Each product is appended as a dictionary.

9. nxt = soup.select_one("li.next a")
   Finds the "next page" button. If none exists (last page), break. Otherwise url = urljoin(url, nxt["href"]) and the loop continues. This is the crawling (link-following) part.

10. Printing: first 10 products in a formatted table, then the total count.

11. csv.DictWriter -> writes header and all rows to products.csv.

PIPELINE: fetch page -> parse -> extract products -> find next link -> repeat -> save CSV.

