VIVA Q&A
Q: What is a web crawler?
A: A program that automatically visits pages, extracts data and follows links to find more pages. Search engines use crawlers to collect pages.

Q: Crawler vs scraper?
A: Crawler discovers and follows links. Scraper extracts specific data. This program does both.

Q: Libraries used?
A: requests (fetch HTML) and BeautifulSoup (parse). Scrapy is a full framework alternative.

Q: What does BeautifulSoup do?
A: Parses HTML into a tree searchable with tags and CSS selectors (select, select_one, find).

Q: What is a CSS selector? Example?
A: A pattern to pick elements. "article.product_pod" = article tags with class product_pod.

Q: select vs select_one?
A: select returns a list of all matches; select_one returns the first match.

Q: How does it reach the next page?
A: Reads the href of "li.next a", joins it to the current URL with urljoin, loops.

Q: Why urljoin?
A: Page links are relative (page-2.html); urljoin makes complete URLs.

Q: What is robots.txt?
A: A file telling crawlers which parts of a site not to visit. A polite crawler obeys it and adds delays between requests.

Q: Why this website?
A: It is a sandbox site meant for scraping practice, so it is legal and safe.

Q: What is the crawl frontier?
A: The queue of URLs still to visit. A full crawler uses a queue (BFS) plus a visited set to avoid repeats.

Q: Why timeout=10?
A: Avoids waiting forever if the server doesn't respond.

Q: What if the page uses JavaScript to load products?
A: requests only gets raw HTML. Use Selenium or Playwright.

Q: Ethical/legal points?
A: Respect robots.txt and terms of service, rate-limit requests, avoid personal data.

Q: What does status code 200 / 404 mean?
A: 200 = OK. 404 = page not found. Use resp.status_code or resp.raise_for_status() to check.

